@section 26.3 | Migration and Urban Society

@q 17:80-86

@table
Migration term | Meaning
**Population implosion** | **Philip M. Hauser**'s term for people crowding into cities
Push-pull **migration theory** | **Everett Lee** (1966); Ravenstein's laws (1885)
Largest migration stream in India | **Rural to rural** (mostly **women** moving on marriage), not rural to urban
**Urban corridors** | Strips of growth along transport routes; expanding urban functions
Indus Project | India-**USA** joint project on child labour (not Canada)
@end

Northern and western Indian cities have **male-heavy** sex ratios from male migration; women's work participation is higher in the south because of **high literacy**, not a small Christian share, so statement 3 in Q80 is wrong.

@section 26.4 | Urban Problems

@q 17:87-93

@table
Slum fact (2011) | Figure
Slum population | **6.5 crore** (17.4% of urban population)
Largest slum population (state) | **Maharashtra**, then Andhra Pradesh, West Bengal, UP
Most slum-reporting towns | **Tamil Nadu**
City with most slum people (2001, among options) | **Delhi** (Greater Mumbai overall)
Effects of rapid urbanization | **Slums**, crowding in big cities, **falling service quality**, crime; infrastructure does **not** keep pace
@end

The planned new city "between Mumbai and Ahmedabad" refers to the **Delhi-Mumbai Industrial Corridor** cities; Maharashtra is not the most urbanized state.

@section 26.5 | Urban Missions

@q 17:94-98

@src1 17:14
@src1 17:15
@one U.P. R.O./A.R.O. (Mains) 2013
Which one of the following is not true for Jawahar Lal Nehru National Urban Renewal Mission? It was :
@ans a 10 year programme
@end

@one U.P. U.D.A./L.D.A. (Spl.) (Pre) 2010
JNNURM is concerned with improving which of the following?
@ans Urban Infrastructure
@end

@q 17:99-100

@table
Urban mission | Year | Detail
**JNNURM** | **December 2005** | 7 years (to 2012, extended to 2014), not 10; urban infrastructure, transport, sanitation, heritage areas, slum services; not electrification
**Smart Cities Mission** | **25 June 2015** | **100 cities**; water and sewerage financed partly by **property tax**; aims: good governance, clean and green city, smart mobility
AMRUT / AMRUT 2.0 | 2015 / 2021 | Water supply and sewerage in 500 cities
HRIDAY | 2015 | **Heritage cities**
**India Urban Observatory** | **2019**, **New Delhi** | Real-time city data (Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs)
Urban infrastructure | **Water, sanitation, transport**; housing is a separate sector
@end

@up UP link: UP has **7 million-plus cities** (2011), with **Kanpur and Lucknow** the largest; **Ghaziabad** is the densest. Ten UP cities are in the **Smart Cities Mission** (Lucknow, Kanpur, Agra, Varanasi, Prayagraj, Aligarh, Jhansi, Moradabad, Bareilly, Saharanpur). The planned **Noida-Greater Noida-Jewar** belt is UP's fastest-growing urban corridor.

@trap
**Class I = 1 lakh+; small towns = Class IV-VI.** **Million-plus: 35 (2001), 53 (2011).** **6 megacities in 2001 (no Ahmedabad).** **Mumbai > Delhi > Kolkata.** **Kolkata first million city (1901).** **UP: 6 million cities in 2001, 7 in 2011.** **Hauser = population implosion; Lee = migration theory.** **Rural-to-rural = largest stream (women).** **JNNURM 2005, 7 years.** **Smart Cities 2015, 100 cities, property tax.** **India Urban Observatory: New Delhi.**
@end

